    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, the teacher considers: Did all groups demonstrate in their project presentations that they can link electron arrangement to formula — or did some groups focus only on memorised formulae without explanation? Were the two-sentence DQB final answers causal (electron arrangement causes valency causes formula) or merely descriptive (CAN has calcium and nitrogen)? Which learners still conflate the subscript in a formula with the number of atoms of the element rather than with the valency ratio? How might the New Wonderings on the DQB be used as entry points for Sub-strand 2.2 or for cross-curricular work with Agriculture? Were locally available materials (bottle tops, wire, clay) sufficient for meaningful model building, or does the school need to source additional resources before the next cohort runs this unit? The teacher also notes whether the careers discussion was substantive or rushed — if rushed, a dedicated five-minute careers integration moment should be built into Lesson 7 next time.",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "Learners observed electron-configuration models built from bottle tops, wire, and clay representing the first 20 elements; compound-formula charts showing names, formulae, and valency justifications for Kenyan compounds including CAN and DAP; and the completed Driving Question Board with sticky-note predictions from Lesson 1 alongside final two-sentence resolutions.",
      "learned": "Learners established that the inner structure of an atom — specifically the number of electrons in the outer shell — determines how many electrons an atom loses, gains, or shares, producing a specific ion charge called valency; that valency values are cross-multiplied to produce the only chemically correct formula for a compound; and that the formulae Ca(NO3)2 · NH4NO3 for CAN and (NH4)2HPO4 for DAP are therefore not arbitrary labels but necessary consequences of the electron arrangements of calcium, nitrogen, hydrogen, oxygen, and phosphorus.",
      "explained": "Learners explained that: a tea farmer in Kericho sees different formulae on her fertiliser bags because calcium (Group 2, arrangement 2,8,8,2) always forms Ca2+ while ammonium carries a 1+ charge and nitrate a 1- charge and phosphate a 3- charge; cross-multiplying these valencies produces the fixed, unalterable ratios encoded in the formulae; balanced equations verify that no atoms are created or destroyed in these fixed-ratio combinations; and therefore the specific formula on each bag is a direct, readable record of the electron structure of the atoms inside — making the periodic table the key to understanding Kenyan agriculture at the molecular level."
    }
