      "learned": "Students learned that metallic elements in Groups 1, 2, and 3 lose electrons equal to their group number to form positively charged cations (e.g., Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺), while non-metallic elements in Groups 6 and 7 gain electrons to form negatively charged anions (e.g., O²⁻, Cl⁻). The dot-and-cross diagram is a tool to represent this electron transfer visually, showing the full outer shells achieved. Pedagogical note: Emphasise that the charge on the ion (1+, 2+, etc.) is precisely the valency used to write fertiliser formulae — making this lesson a direct building block for Lesson 5.",
